{"id":"pad","cards":[{"id":"pad7485baac","l":1,"f":"<b>Plaque cascade</b>?","b":"(1) <b>endothelial injury</b> (chemical/mechanical) → inflammation, lipid and WBCs gather beneath the endothelium → (2) macrophages engulf lipid → <b>foam cells → fatty streak</b> → (3) <b>fibrous plaque</b> (inflammatory cells, smooth muscle, fibroblasts, calcium) → (4) <b>progressive stenosis</b> → (5) <b>plaque rupture &amp; thrombosis</b> (platelet adhesion → occlusion).","c":"Atherosclerosis — pathophysiology"},{"id":"pad795f0692","l":1,"f":"<b>Antiplatelets act only at step 5</b>?","b":"they prevent platelet thrombosis but don't treat the plaque itself.","c":"Atherosclerosis — pathophysiology"},{"id":"pad6424a38b","l":1,"f":"<b>Non-modifiable</b> — list them","b":"Increasing age · Male sex · Family history / genetics","c":"Atherosclerosis — pathophysiology"},{"id":"pad2f3fb06f","l":1,"f":"<b>Modifiable — MAJOR (memorize)</b> — list them","b":"<b>Diabetes mellitus</b> · <b>Smoking</b> · <b>Hypertension</b> · <b>Dyslipidaemia</b>","c":"Atherosclerosis — pathophysiology"},{"id":"pad9f6b4181","l":1,"f":"<b>Modifiable — additional</b> — list them","b":"Obesity · Physical inactivity · Insulin resistance","c":"Atherosclerosis — pathophysiology"},{"id":"pad445688d6","l":2,"f":"The most important <b>modifiable major</b> risk factor among the options","b":"<b>diabetes mellitus</b> (obesity is minor; family history and male sex aren't modifiable)","c":"Atherosclerosis — pathophysiology"},{"id":"pad02754382","l":1,"f":"<b>PAD is a marker of systemic atherosclerosis</b>?","b":"associated with coronary and cerebrovascular disease, MI, stroke and increased mortality. <b>MI is the more frequent event</b>; cardiovascular events and death are commoner than amputation. Leg pulses are a window onto arterial disease that coronaries can't give.","c":"PAD — significance & prognosis"},{"id":"padc369cedc","l":1,"f":"<b>Spectrum</b>?","b":"asymptomatic (the majority) → <b>intermittent claudication</b> → <b>CLTI</b>: ischaemic rest pain → ulceration/tissue loss → gangrene.","c":"Clinical presentation"},{"id":"pad999e9c2b","l":1,"f":"<b>No claudication ≠ no PAD</b>?","b":"limited activity can hide ischaemia.","c":"Clinical presentation"},{"id":"padb6d75415","l":1,"f":"<b>Aortoiliac / common iliac</b> — Claudication site?","b":"<b>Buttock / gluteal</b>","c":"Clinical presentation › Intermittent claudication"},{"id":"padd93db485","l":1,"f":"<b>Aortoiliac / common iliac</b> — Why?","b":"Lesion above the internal iliac origin → gluteal muscles under-perfused","c":"Clinical presentation › Intermittent claudication"},{"id":"padb92217aa","l":1,"f":"<b>External iliac / proximal femoral</b> — Claudication site?","b":"<b>Thigh</b>","c":"Clinical presentation › Intermittent claudication"},{"id":"pad9f0b015d","l":1,"f":"<b>External iliac / proximal femoral</b> — Why?","b":"Internal iliac intact → gluteal perfusion preserved","c":"Clinical presentation › Intermittent claudication"},{"id":"padabf09628","l":1,"f":"<b>More distal (SFA, popliteal, tibial)</b> — Claudication site?","b":"<b>Calf</b>","c":"Clinical presentation › Intermittent claudication"},{"id":"pad55ecd645","l":1,"f":"<b>More distal (SFA, popliteal, tibial)</b> — Why?","b":"Only the distal muscles are ischaemic","c":"Clinical presentation › Intermittent claudication"},{"id":"padd12f0a60","l":1,"f":"<b>Vascular claudication</b> — Cause?","b":"Insufficient arterial supply to exercising muscle","c":"Clinical presentation › Intermittent claudication"},{"id":"pad31513dd8","l":1,"f":"<b>Neurogenic claudication</b> — Cause?","b":"Neural compression (spinal stenosis)","c":"Clinical presentation › Intermittent claudication"},{"id":"padba7ced5c","l":1,"f":"<b>Vascular claudication</b> — Pattern?","b":"<b>Reproducible</b> exertional pain","c":"Clinical presentation › Intermittent claudication"},{"id":"padb3436e30","l":1,"f":"<b>Neurogenic claudication</b> — Pattern?","b":"Often <b>posture-dependent</b>","c":"Clinical presentation › Intermittent claudication"},{"id":"pad7490de22","l":1,"f":"<b>Vascular claudication</b> — Relief?","b":"<b>Stopping/rest — no change of position needed</b>","c":"Clinical presentation › Intermittent claudication"},{"id":"pad26a7c015","l":1,"f":"<b>Neurogenic claudication</b> — Relief?","b":"May need <b>sitting or spinal flexion</b>","c":"Clinical presentation › Intermittent claudication"},{"id":"pad254f4bc6","l":2,"f":"Diabetic, leg pain and numbness after <b>500 m</b>, must <b>stop and rest</b> to relieve it, normal neuro exam","b":"<b>vascular claudication</b>","c":"Clinical presentation › Intermittent claudication"},{"id":"pad0e99904a","l":2,"f":"Most common cause of <b>calf claudication in a 20-year-old non-smoker</b>","b":"<b>popliteal entrapment syndrome</b>","c":"Clinical presentation › Intermittent claudication"},{"id":"pad632d2755","l":1,"f":"<b>Elevation pallor</b> — What you see?","b":"Foot turns pale when raised","c":"Physical examination"},{"id":"pade358e368","l":1,"f":"<b>Elevation pallor</b> — Meaning?","b":"Inadequate arterial perfusion","c":"Physical examination"},{"id":"padbc7a686f","l":1,"f":"<b>Dependent rubor</b> — What you see?","b":"Foot turns red when lowered","c":"Physical examination"},{"id":"pad01f1e5e6","l":1,"f":"<b>Dependent rubor</b> — Meaning?","b":"Dilated ischaemic vessels + dependent filling","c":"Physical examination"},{"id":"pad21d27863","l":1,"f":"<b>Ulcer</b> — What you see?","b":"Tissue defect with a visible base; may expose bone","c":"Physical examination"},{"id":"padc0e713ef","l":1,"f":"<b>Ulcer</b> — Meaning?","b":"Tissue loss from chronic ischaemia","c":"Physical examination"},{"id":"pad23b6a3ef","l":1,"f":"<b>Gangrene</b> — What you see?","b":"Tissue necrosis","c":"Physical examination"},{"id":"padc1674b5e","l":1,"f":"<b>Gangrene</b> — Meaning?","b":"Advanced ischaemia","c":"Physical examination"},{"id":"padba1872ce","l":2,"f":"Sign supporting a non-healing <b>ischaemic</b> ulcer","b":"<b>between the 1st and 2nd toes, tender, with poor granulation tissue</b>","c":"Physical examination"},{"id":"pad1bf21e65","l":1,"f":"<b>Worked example</b>?","b":"brachial 100 and 120 → denominator 120. Ankle 120 and 100 → ABI = 120/120 = <b>1.0</b>; ankle 50 and 60 → 60/120 = <b>0.5</b>.","c":"Investigations › Ankle–brachial index (ABI)"},{"id":"paddc8f8830","l":1,"f":"<b>&gt; 1.40</b> — Meaning?","b":"<b>Non-compressible (calcified) arteries — falsely high</b>, not excellent perfusion (values of ~1.5, even ~2)","c":"Investigations › Ankle–brachial index (ABI)"},{"id":"pad3d4df447","l":1,"f":"<b>1.00 – 1.40</b> — Meaning?","b":"<b>Normal</b>","c":"Investigations › Ankle–brachial index (ABI)"},{"id":"pad0b40bebc","l":1,"f":"<b>0.91 – 0.99</b> — Meaning?","b":"Borderline","c":"Investigations › Ankle–brachial index (ABI)"},{"id":"padc91259d0","l":1,"f":"<b>≤ 0.90</b> — Meaning?","b":"<b>PAD</b> — the lower, the more severe and the worse the cardiovascular risk","c":"Investigations › Ankle–brachial index (ABI)"},{"id":"padc75157a2","l":2,"f":"How to calculate the ABI","b":"<b>ankle systolic BP / brachial systolic BP</b>","c":"Investigations › Ankle–brachial index (ABI)"},{"id":"pad6441e9fa","l":2,"f":"Calf claudication at 500 m, palpable femoral pulse, absent pulses below","b":"next vascular test <b>ankle–brachial index</b>","c":"Investigations › Ankle–brachial index (ABI)"},{"id":"pad92eb05da","l":1,"f":"<b>Duplex US</b> — Shows?","b":"Structure + flow velocity/waveforms","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"padfaf11745","l":1,"f":"<b>CT angiography</b> — Shows?","b":"Detailed anatomy, 3D","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pad37413a8b","l":1,"f":"<b>MR angiography</b> — Shows?","b":"Arterial anatomy","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"padf00a125a","l":1,"f":"<b>Catheter angiography</b> — Shows?","b":"Stenosis/occlusion, distal flow","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pad1b14f315","l":1,"f":"<b>Duplex US</b> — Radiation/contrast?","b":"Neither","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"padde126a3f","l":1,"f":"<b>CT angiography</b> — Radiation/contrast?","b":"Radiation + IV contrast","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"padde5db21c","l":1,"f":"<b>MR angiography</b> — Radiation/contrast?","b":"No ionizing radiation","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"padb00b28bf","l":1,"f":"<b>Catheter angiography</b> — Radiation/contrast?","b":"Invasive arterial access","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pad21660fe3","l":1,"f":"<b>Duplex US</b> — Practicalities?","b":"Cheap, painless","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pad817a85f3","l":1,"f":"<b>CT angiography</b> — Practicalities?","b":"Fast (~5 min), less operator-dependent","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pade1ffdc2e","l":1,"f":"<b>MR angiography</b> — Practicalities?","b":"Expensive, slow, motion-sensitive","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pad4b37508c","l":1,"f":"<b>Catheter angiography</b> — Practicalities?","b":"Procedure-based","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pad44b977b7","l":1,"f":"<b>Duplex US</b> — Limitation?","b":"<b>Operator-dependent</b>","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pad9e3afcfe","l":1,"f":"<b>CT angiography</b> — Limitation?","b":"Contrast and radiation","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pad4b40fcec","l":1,"f":"<b>MR angiography</b> — Limitation?","b":"Less practical in some","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pada014a3ab","l":1,"f":"<b>Catheter angiography</b> — Limitation?","b":"Invasive","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pad52b162a8","l":1,"f":"<b>Duplex US</b> — Best use?","b":"Uncertain findings; characterize disease","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pada448ffa4","l":1,"f":"<b>CT angiography</b> — Best use?","b":"<b>Planning revascularization</b>","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pad645b3f80","l":1,"f":"<b>MR angiography</b> — Best use?","b":"Imaging without radiation","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pad3d0f0014","l":1,"f":"<b>Catheter angiography</b> — Best use?","b":"<b>When an endovascular procedure is planned</b>","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pada066456b","l":1,"f":"<b>Anatomy</b>?","b":"aorta → common iliac → internal iliac (pelvis, gluteal, penile) + <b>external iliac → common femoral</b> (under the inguinal ligament) → <b>profunda femoris</b> (thigh muscles) + <b>superficial femoral</b> → popliteal → anterior tibial, posterior tibial, peroneal.","c":"Investigations › Imaging"},{"id":"pad846b84da","l":1,"f":"<b>Goals</b>?","b":"↓ <b>MACE</b> (MI, stroke, CV death) and ↓ <b>MALE</b> (major amputation, serious limb events); prolong survival, relieve symptoms, save the limb, improve quality of life. <b>Every plan treats both the systemic risk and the limb.</b>","c":"Management"},{"id":"pad7c63841f","l":1,"f":"<b>Treatment map</b>?","b":"<b>smoking cessation (very important)</b> · aggressive control of <b>diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidaemia</b> · diet, weight, exercise · <b>antiplatelet</b> · <b>statin for all PAD — even with normal cholesterol</b> · <b>supervised exercise</b> · <b>revascularization for selected patients only (CLTI or refractory claudication)</b>.","c":"Management"},{"id":"pad4f001ac8","l":1,"f":"<b>Supervised exercise</b>?","b":"<b>≥ 3 times/week, ~30 min per session</b> — walk until claudication, continue into tolerable pain, <b>rest ~3–4 min</b>, repeat. Builds collaterals, muscle conditioning and walking efficiency.","c":"Management"},{"id":"pad3599f710","l":2,"f":"Calf claudication relieved by rest, <b>ABI 0.60/0.65</b>","b":"<b>best medical therapy and supervised exercise</b> first","c":"Management"},{"id":"padc432a77d","l":2,"f":"What decreases mortality/progression in <b>Buerger's disease</b>","b":"<b>smoking cessation</b>","c":"Management"},{"id":"pad2a2f832e","l":1,"f":"<b>Revascularize when</b>?","b":"<b>CLTI — rest pain, ulcers or tissue loss — with a salvageable limb</b>, or <b>lifestyle-limiting claudication despite medical therapy and structured exercise</b>.","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"pad76d7ede1","l":1,"f":"<b>Avoid early intervention for uncomplicated claudication</b>?","b":"many improve conservatively; repairs have limited durability; continued smoking worsens results.","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"pad0afc6f9d","l":1,"f":"<b>Endovascular (angioplasty ± stent)</b> — Best for?","b":"<b>Short/focal lesions</b>","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"pad356ebe2a","l":1,"f":"<b>Surgical bypass</b> — Best for?","b":"<b>Long/complex lesions</b>, unfavourable anatomy, failed endovascular","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"pade7289682","l":1,"f":"<b>Endovascular (angioplasty ± stent)</b> — Invasiveness / risk?","b":"Lower","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"pad36b09df5","l":1,"f":"<b>Surgical bypass</b> — Invasiveness / risk?","b":"Higher — more incisions, more perioperative risk","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"pad13bd6053","l":1,"f":"<b>Endovascular (angioplasty ± stent)</b> — Recovery?","b":"Faster","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"padb450897a","l":1,"f":"<b>Surgical bypass</b> — Recovery?","b":"Slower","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"pad0ec7ea71","l":1,"f":"<b>Endovascular (angioplasty ± stent)</b> — Durability?","b":"Often lower — restenosis, repeat procedures","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"pad79f7a67c","l":1,"f":"<b>Surgical bypass</b> — Durability?","b":"Often higher, <b>especially with autologous vein</b>","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"pad72d78ee7","l":1,"f":"<b>Angioplasty</b>?","b":"guidewire across the lesion → balloon compresses the plaque (risk of <b>recoil/re-narrowing</b>) → <b>stent</b> holds the lumen","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"pad1aa59c66","l":1,"f":"<b>Bypass</b>?","b":"from a patent artery above the block to a suitable artery below — autologous vein or prosthetic graft","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"pad38fa8fb8","l":2,"f":"Bypass rather than conservative management","b":"<b>rest pain with gangrene of the toes</b> (CLTI) — not claudication &gt; 100 m, not a well-perfused or neuropathic ulcer","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"pad5484d9b6","l":2,"f":"Surgical rather than conservative","b":"<b>rest pain with gangrene of the toe</b> (fragmentary recall)","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"padc07c6017","l":2,"f":"Diabetic with a <b>1 × 2 cm forefoot ulcer</b> and pain → keyed <b>wound care and revascularization</b> (both accepted); besides wound care, the most important step","b":"<b>revascularization</b>","c":"Management › Revascularization"},{"id":"padf1e32f4d","l":1,"f":"<b>Potassium + acids/lactate</b> — Systemic effect?","b":"<b>Hyperkalaemia, metabolic acidosis → arrhythmias</b>","c":"Irreversible ischaemia & amputation"},{"id":"pad4671b129","l":1,"f":"<b>Myoglobin</b> — Systemic effect?","b":"<b>Acute kidney injury</b>","c":"Irreversible ischaemia & amputation"},{"id":"pad94cb9990","l":1,"f":"<b>Toxic metabolites</b> — Systemic effect?","b":"<b>ARDS</b>, systemic instability","c":"Irreversible ischaemia & amputation"}]}